  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Welcome to Season Three of Kukhnya. At the French restaurant Claude Monet in Moscow, Max Lavrov has traded his chef jacket for a waiter's vest, and he quickly discovers that serving demanding guests in the dining room is harder than it looks.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я Макс Лавров.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Раньше работал поваром, теперь я официант.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Работать официантом совсем не легко.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Вы сильно заблуждаетесь.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Здравствуйте!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Вы записываете?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я запомню.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Гратен, пожалуйста. А мне кролика.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Салат и вода без газа.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "И апельсиновый фреш.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Что это?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Красота!",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through their conversation line by line and unpack the Russian phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я Макс Лавров.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I am Max Lavrov.' Max anchors the line on 'I', while 'Lavrov' completes the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Раньше работал поваром, теперь я официант.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I used to work as a cook, now I am a waiter.' The meaning hinges on 'earlier' working together with 'waiter' in spoken Russian.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Работать официантом совсем не легко.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Working as a waiter is not easy at all.' The negative particle sits right before the verb meaning 'to work' to negate the statement.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Вы сильно заблуждаетесь.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You are gravely mistaken.' In Russian, the idea of 'you' flows naturally into 'are mistaken' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Здравствуйте!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Hello!' In Russian, the idea of 'hello' flows naturally into 'hello' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Вы записываете?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Are you writing this down?' In Russian, the idea of 'you' flows naturally into 'write down' without extra filler words. Max answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я запомню.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I will remember it.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'I' before landing on 'will remember'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Гратен, пожалуйста. А мне кролика.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Gratin, please. And rabbit for me.' the guest brings out the contrast between 'gratin' and 'please' in a few short words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Салат и вода без газа.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'A salad and still water.' the guest anchors the line on 'salad', while 'gas' completes the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "И апельсиновый фреш.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'And fresh orange juice.' The grammar packs 'and' and 'fresh juice' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Что это?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'What is this?' The meaning hinges on 'what' working together with 'this' in spoken Russian.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Красота!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Beautiful!' In Russian, the idea of 'beauty' flows naturally into 'beauty' without extra filler words. Alright, let's hear the entire conversation one more time from start to finish.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
